  SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', 'isolated-domain-activity-implies-cellular-signaling' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'attributes-coupling-defect-to-binding-affinity' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', 'confuses-transduction-step-with-catalytic-active-site' FROM q;

-- Q17 [G Protein-Coupled Receptors] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Non-enzymatic Protein Function and Protein Analysis', 'G Protein-Coupled Receptors', 'A pharmacologist studies a hormone that acts through a G protein-coupled receptor. In a membrane preparation, she adds the hormone along with GTPγS, a synthetic guanine nucleotide analog that cannot be hydrolyzed. Compared with a preparation given the hormone plus ordinary GTP, the GTPγS preparation shows downstream signaling that persists long after the hormone is washed away. Which change in the heterotrimeric G protein best accounts for this prolonged signal?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The α subunit remains locked in its nucleotide-bound active state because the analog cannot be cleaved"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The βγ complex retains the analog, and its inability to release it sustains effector activation"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The α subunit is held in its nucleotide-bound inactive state, preventing it from resetting the receptor"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The receptor itself retains the analog in its cytoplasmic domain, keeping the ligand site continuously occupied"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Biosignaling.'' The answer is A because the α subunit is the guanine-nucleotide-binding subunit, and its GTP-bound form is the active species; a non-hydrolyzable analog cannot be cleaved, so the α subunit stays continuously active and keeps driving downstream signaling. (Choice A) is correct: in a heterotrimeric G protein the α subunit binds guanine nucleotides and possesses GTPase activity, with the GTP-bound form being active and the GDP-bound form inactive—blocking hydrolysis with GTPγS traps α in the active state, exactly like activating point mutations that "prevent GTP cleavage leave the protein continually activated." (Choice B) is incorrect: this reflects the misconception that the βγ complex is the nucleotide-binding subunit, but it is the α subunit—not βγ—that binds and hydrolyzes guanine nucleotides. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the active/inactive relationship; the nucleotide-bound state that is trapped by a non-hydrolyzable analog corresponds to the active (GTP-bound) form, not the inactive (GDP-bound) form. (Choice D) is incorrect: this misassigns the guanine-nucleotide-binding role to the receptor, but the GPCR is the transducer that activates the G protein, and it is the G protein''s α subunit—not the receptor—that binds the nucleotide. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the α subunit of a heterotrimeric G protein binds guanine nucleotides and is active when GTP-bound.', 'medium', '3B', 'Biosignaling', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
  SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong subunit binds guanine nucleotide' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'GDP-bound treated as active state' FROM q
  UNION ALL
  SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', 'receptor mistaken for the nucleotide-binding transducer' FROM q;

-- Q18 [G Protein-Coupled Receptors] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Non-enzymatic Protein Function and Protein Analysis', 'G Protein-Coupled Receptors', 'A researcher engineers a stimulatory G protein whose \(\alpha\) subunit has a point mutation in its catalytic pocket that abolishes the subunit''s ability to hydrolyze bound GTP, though GTP binding and effector activation are unaffected. A single brief pulse of hormone is applied to activate the receptor, and the hormone is then completely washed away so the receptor returns to its resting conformation. Compared with a cell expressing wild-type \(G\alpha\), what is the most likely downstream effect on the effector enzyme (adenylyl cyclase) after the wash?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Adenylyl cyclase remains persistently active long after the hormone is gone, because the mutant \\(\\alpha\\) subunit stays locked in its GTP-bound signaling state."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Adenylyl cyclase shuts off faster than in wild-type cells, because the mutant \\(\\alpha\\) subunit can no longer bind GTP and reassociates immediately with the \\(\\beta\\gamma\\) dimer."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Adenylyl cyclase is never activated at all, because a subunit that cannot hydrolyze GTP is unable to dissociate from \\(\\beta\\gamma\\) and reach the effector."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Adenylyl cyclase is activated normally during the pulse and then returns to baseline on the usual timescale once the hormone is washed away."}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This Biosignaling item requires predicting the output of a G protein-coupled receptor cascade when the intrinsic timing switch of the \(\alpha\) subunit is broken. In the normal cycle, an agonist-bound receptor catalyzes GDP-for-GTP exchange on \(G\alpha\); the GTP-bound \(\alpha\) subunit dissociates from \(\beta\gamma\) and activates its effector, and signaling is terminated only when the subunit''s own intrinsic GTPase hydrolyzes GTP back to GDP, restoring the inactive \(\alpha\)-GDP state that reassociates with \(\beta\gamma\). Because hydrolysis is the sole reset step, it—not the presence of hormone—sets how long the effector stays on. (Choice A) is correct: with hydrolysis abolished but GTP binding and effector activation intact, the pulse loads the mutant \(\alpha\) subunit with GTP, and it cannot return to the off state; it therefore keeps stimulating adenylyl cyclase long after the hormone is washed away and the receptor has reset (the same logic underlies cholera toxin''s locking of \(G_s\alpha\) in its active form). (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the mechanism—the lesion blocks GTP hydrolysis, not GTP binding, and it is the GTP-loaded (not GDP-loaded) state that dissociates from \(\beta\gamma\), so the mutant signals longer, not shorter. (Choice C) is incorrect: the stem specifies that GTP binding and effector activation are unaffected, so the subunit still dissociates and activates the cyclase during the pulse; the defect lies only in turning the signal off. (Choice D) is incorrect: normal return to baseline depends on GTP hydrolysis to end the signal, which is exactly the step this mutant lacks, so the effector cannot shut off on the usual timescale. Skill 2: the student must apply the GTP/GDP switch mechanism to a novel mutant scenario and predict the downstream consequence rather than recall a stated fact.', 'hard', '3B', 'Biosignaling', 'biochemistry', 2, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
